Simple Top Viewer
-----------------

This is a simple package for viewing the 'top' output of multiple machines on a single webpage.

It works as follows:

* it assumes all machines have access to the same directory (e.g. NFS mounted)
* crontab.txt contains a crontab entry that needs to be set on each monitored machine (no root required)
* stats.py is run by crontab on each machine separately, it writes the output of 'top' to a <machine>.dat file (in the shared directory)
* index.php reads all .dat files and shows an overview in HTML


This code is a dump from what has been running in [our research group](http://dtai.cs.kuleuven.be/) for years. Many things are configurable but currently inlined, as that is how the code grew.


How to test
-----------
* Unpack in a working folder of your (php-aware) webserver
* run ./stats.py to generate at least one .dat file
* browse to the correct location on your webserver

Configuration
-------------
Site-specific settings (page title, machine families, hardware specs, banner
link) live in config.local.php, which is git-ignored so they stay out of the
public repository. Copy config.example.php to config.local.php and edit it;
without one, index.php falls back to generic defaults. stats.py needs no
configuration: it writes its .dat file next to itself.

Accurate per-user disk usage (optional, needs root)
---------------------------------------------------
stats.py opportunistically triggers diskusage.py to chart per-user usage of
local data disks. Run as a regular user it cannot descend into other users'
unreadable directories, so part of each disk shows up unattributed. The
optional root-side pipeline fixes that, without ever letting root write to a
user-controlled path:

* install-disk-usage (run as root on each machine, or on all of them at once
  with ./deploy-disk-usage machine1 machine2 ...) installs root-owned copies
  of diskusage.py and the update-disk-usage wrapper into /root/bin.
* a root cron job (overnight window, further gated so it only scans when the
  cached result is stale AND the machine is near-idle) runs the scan with
  full visibility and writes <machine>.du to a root-owned LOCAL directory,
  /var/lib/disk-usage. Root never writes into the shared web directory, so
  nobody can redirect the root job's output with a planted symlink.
* an unprivileged cron job copies the .du into the web directory whenever a
  newer scan exists — atomically, timestamps preserved. If the root pipeline
  ever breaks, the published file simply ages out and stats.py's user-level
  fallback scan takes over again.

With root visibility, everything on a monitored filesystem gets attributed:
per-user data directories by name, and everything else — the OS install,
stray files — by file owner, so the system itself shows up as 'root' rather
than as an anonymous remainder. Users below the display threshold are pooled
into a single '(small users)' entry. What stays unattributed after that is
essentially deleted-but-open files and filesystem metadata.

Nothing in the pipeline is site-specific: at install time the installer
derives the web directory (the one it sits in) and the publishing user (that
directory's owner) and substitutes them into the installed copies.

Improvements and patches welcome.
